WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.240
We have noses to smell,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:06.440
eyes to see, and ears to hear, right?

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.690
Well, actually, your ears are responsible

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.120
for much more than just hearing.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.990
In fact, they can even help you taste.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.722
And that's all thanks to what's inside.

00:00:17.722 --> 00:00:20.720
We think of ears as
these flappy appendages

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.780
that stick out of our heads.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.438
But the most important structures

00:00:24.438 --> 00:00:27.145
are actually on the inside,

00:00:27.145 --> 00:00:29.930
and they're known as the
inner and middle ear.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.200
Iram Ahmad: Inside your middle ear

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.460
is the nerve that moves
the muscles of your face.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.790
Narrator: Aka the facial nerve.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:38.980
And if it becomes inflamed or injured,

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.410
say, from a nasty ear infection,

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.990
you can lose control of
the muscles in your face.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.240
But it could actually get worse.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:48.960
Ahmad: Now, along that facial nerve

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.167
there is a taste nerve.

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:52.900
It's called chorda tympani.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.930
Narrator: That's right,
the nerve for taste

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.510
runs right through your middle ear.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.550
And if that nerve gets
damaged, you could alter

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.270
or even lose your sense
of taste permanently.

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:05.970
By the way, not only are your ears

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.868
incredibly important for tasting food,

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.090
but what you're listening to

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.090
can also change your food's flavor.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.790
For example, research has
shown that white noise

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.550
can dull saltiness but
enhance crunchiness.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.590
And in one study, volunteers
perceived a dish to be eggier

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:27.380
when they listened to the
sound of clucking chickens.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.510
But whether it's the sound of chickens

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.970
or my beautiful voice,

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.870
it all enters your ear
through the cochlea,

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:38.230
a shell-like structure in the inner ear.

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:42.733
Inside the cochlea there are
over 16,000 hairlike cells,

00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:45.050
which take vibrations entering your ear

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:48.283
and convert them into nerve impulses.

00:01:48.283 --> 00:01:50.590
Those impulses travel to your brain,

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.650
which turns them into the sound you hear.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.060
So if it weren't for those
cells, you'd be deaf.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.560
Which is why it's incredibly important

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.470
that you keep them protected.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.050
Especially from menaces like bacteria,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.480
viruses, and even adventurous insects.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:11.249
And for that, your ears'
best defense is earwax.

00:02:11.249 --> 00:02:13.550
Ahmad: Earwax is good.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.370
It is not bad to have earwax.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:19.292
Narrator: That's right. You
need that gross, golden wax.

00:02:19.292 --> 00:02:22.050
It not only has antibacterial properties,

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.270
but it's also downright sticky,

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.470
helping to stop invaders
right in their tracks.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.960
But earwax isn't the only
part of your inner ear

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.312
that keeps you healthy.

00:02:31.312 --> 00:02:33.559
Ahmad: So, there is a tube made of muscle

00:02:33.559 --> 00:02:36.900
that connects the back of
your nose and your ears.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.040
Narrator: It's called the eustachian tube,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.690
and it helps equalize pressure

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.450
between your ears and the
atmosphere around you.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.130
Normally the tube is sealed off

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.786
so nothing can travel between
your ears and your throat,

00:02:48.786 --> 00:02:50.720
which keeps infections at bay.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.650
But when there's a change in pressure,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.840
like when you're taking
off in an airplane,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.840
the air inside the tube expands.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.729
Which typically makes you
want to pop your ears.

00:03:00.729 --> 00:03:02.245
And that's a good thing too,

00:03:02.245 --> 00:03:03.645
because popping your ears

00:03:03.645 --> 00:03:06.220
opens the tube and releases the air,

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.210
balancing the pressure inside and out.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.754
Speaking of balance, there's one other

00:03:11.754 --> 00:03:13.575
incredibly important structure

00:03:13.575 --> 00:03:16.216
you can find inside your inner ear:

00:03:16.216 --> 00:03:18.141
the vestibular system.

00:03:18.141 --> 00:03:20.930
Kind of like a level,
it controls your balance

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.940
using a number of liquid-filled canals.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.990
Ahmad: Those balance
canals are responsible

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.520
for telling your body you are moving.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.090
Whether it may be moving left to right

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.020
or your body's moving up and down.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:34.100
Narrator: As you move around,

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.060
fluid in the canals
sloshes against millions

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:40.016
of hairlike structures called stereocilia,

00:03:40.016 --> 00:03:41.920
and this sends a signal to the brain

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.860
where you process it as movement.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.160
That's why infections like the common cold

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.700
can sometimes make you feel dizzy.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.950
They often cause inflammation
in the vestibular nerve,

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.647
which distorts those signals.

00:03:55.647 --> 00:03:57.160
So the next time you find yourself

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.526
walking without falling over,

00:03:59.526 --> 00:04:01.170
or tasting your food,

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.886
or listening to, well, anything,

00:04:03.886 --> 00:04:05.335
think of your ears.

00:04:05.335 --> 00:04:06.700
Which, as it turns out,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:10.504
have a lot more going on inside than out.

